1
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:20,678
The heart of most ultrasound systems is a device called a transducer,

2
00:00:20,774 --> 00:00:24,990
which uses an array of piezoelectric crystals. A piezoelectric crystal

3
00:00:25,030 --> 00:00:28,814
vibrates when an electric signal is applied, producing high frequency sound

4
00:00:28,862 --> 00:00:33,058
pressure waves, which we call ultrasound. More importantly,

5
00:00:33,154 --> 00:00:36,578
this type of crystal can also work in reverse. It can produce

6
00:00:36,634 --> 00:00:40,230
electrical signals when it detects high frequency sound pressure waves.

7
00:00:40,650 --> 00:00:43,618
When a transducer directs ultrasound waves into the body,

8
00:00:43,754 --> 00:00:47,026
they pass right through the skin and into the internal anatomy.

9
00:00:47,218 --> 00:00:50,762
As the waves encounter tissues with different characteristics and densities,

10
00:00:50,906 --> 00:00:54,338
they produce echoes that reflect back to the piezoelectric crystal.

11
00:00:54,474 --> 00:00:57,906
This happens more than a thousand times a second. Returning echoes

12
00:00:57,938 --> 00:01:01,202
are converted to electric signals, which a computer converts into points of

13
00:01:01,226 --> 00:01:04,498
brightness on the image corresponding to the anatomic position and

14
00:01:04,514 --> 00:01:07,834
the strength of the reflecting echoes. A medical transducer

15
00:01:07,882 --> 00:01:11,090
contains a large array of crystals, which allow it to make a series of image

16
00:01:11,130 --> 00:01:14,738
lines that together form a complete image frame called a

17
00:01:14,754 --> 00:01:18,466
sonogram. In addition, all the crystals are repeatedly activated

18
00:01:18,538 --> 00:01:22,474
many times in such a way that a complete image frame is formed around 20

19
00:01:22,522 --> 00:01:25,778
times per second so that real-time motion is displayed in the

20
00:01:25,794 --> 00:01:26,910
ultrasound image.